[
    {
        "content": "<p>What's the best way to investigate a non-reducible <code>rfl</code> these days? Suppose <code>rfl</code> proves a goal but <code>with_reducible rfl</code>does not, and the error is something like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`rfl</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">The</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">left</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">hand</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">side</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">instTopologicalSpaceProd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"n\">is</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">not</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">definitionally</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">equal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">right</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">hand</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">side</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">instTopologicalSpaceProd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>What's the best way to investigate this further? What trick can I still learn? Pretty-printing options? Being more clever with convert or refine?<br>\nFor the record, <code>with_reducible refine Eq.trans (rfl) ?_</code> does not help here.</p>\n<p>(In my motivating case, I could do <code>set_option pp.all true in</code> before the lemma, and manually compare the output. That scales really badly, though. Yes, I could copy this to a file and diff. Same problem, though.)</p>",
        "id": 612415205,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784828592
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think following the trace of <code>set_option trace.Meta.isDefEq true</code> (sometimes with <code>set_option trace.Meta.whnf true</code>) could show you exactly which part is the issue</p>",
        "id": 612417128,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1784829117
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Are you sure you need <code>with_reducible rfl</code>? What matters most often for instance diamonds is <code>with_implicit rfl</code>.</p>",
        "id": 612435364,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1784834894
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>is it not <code>with_reducible_and_instances rfl</code> anymore?</p>",
        "id": 612435790,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784835031
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Indeed, <code>with_implicit</code> would be a better test in my situation. In my case, the difference is about <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ModelProd#doc\">docs#ModelProd</a> (vs Prod), which shouldn't be instance reducible. I do realise now, that I haven't thought about whether it should be implicitly reducible...<br>\nbut let me start a new discussion about that (edit: <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"287929\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Differential.20geometry.20defeq.20abuse/with/612437227\">#mathlib4 &gt; Differential geometry defeq abuse</a> )</p>",
        "id": 612435927,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784835079
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No. A new reducibility level has been introduced since then. Equality of instances is checked at implicit reducibility, so the right incantation is now <code>with_implicit rfl</code>.</p>",
        "id": 612435948,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1784835086
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>~~Is it really still checked at implicit reducibility now that there is the split between <code>implicit_reducible</code> and <code>instance_reducible</code>?<br>\nI thought the point of that split is to make sure that it is specifically not checked at <code>implicit_reducible</code> (so that e.g <code>CategoryTheory.Functor.comp</code> can be <code>implicit_reducible</code> without messing with the instances on different composition orders).~~</p>\n<p>Edit: I was wrong and it’s indeed happening at <code>implicit</code>!</p>",
        "id": 612481903,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Carlier",
        "timestamp": 1784859605
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just for clarification for everyone, because it is a bit confusing at first:<br>\nThere's a distinction between how the <em>value</em> and the <em>type</em> of an instance are treated. The types are compared at instance transparency. This is what prevents a <code>C ((f.comp g).comp h)</code> instance from being used when looking for <code>C (f.comp (g.comp h))</code>.</p>\n<p>But when we check whether the values of two instances are defeq, we do so at implicit transparency, which help to address more diamonds. Instance transparency would be stricter and sometimes too strict.</p>",
        "id": 612536186,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1784885486
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Regarding <code>rfl</code>-failure investigations, I suggest enabling <code>set_option trace.Meta.isDefEq.printTransparency true</code>. Then you see for every defeq sub-problem at which transparency it happens. I find it very useful when debugging such issues.</p>",
        "id": 612536642,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1784885632
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I know that everyone is very busy, but it would be <em>very nice</em> to have a place where everything is explained, with examples and without too much technical details.</p>",
        "id": 612536815,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1784885697
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Point taken. I think the <a href=\"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/Definitions/Recursive-Definitions/#reducibility\">reference manual</a> has a basic toy example about how reducibility interacts with instance synthesis, but it's artificial, and more real-world examples might help. Also, we should mention <code>with_implicit</code> and <code>printTransparency</code> there.</p>",
        "id": 612537806,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1784886009
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If it helps, here's an example which matching instance with <code>with_reducible_and_instances rfl</code> fails but <code>with_implicit rfl</code> works:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">module</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Ring</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">expose</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">implicit_reducible</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">deriving</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Add</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">error: Tactic `rfl` failed: The left-hand side</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  HAdd.hAdd</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">is not definitionally equal to the right-hand side</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  Nat.add</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">⊢ HAdd.hAdd = Nat.add</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">HAdd</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">hAdd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">inferInstance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">with_reducible_and_instances</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">HAdd</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">hAdd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">myNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">inferInstance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">with_implicit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 612539553,
        "sender_full_name": "Edison Xie",
        "timestamp": 1784886577
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"221653\">@Paul Reichert</span> While you're here: for me, the proper fix would be to make the error message more detailed, by expanding implicit arguments as necessary. Here's an example depending on mathlib to illustrate my point:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H'</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModelWithCorners</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModelWithCorners</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H'</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×ˢ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">interior_prod_eq</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">range_prodMap</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">modelWithCorners_prod_coe</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- issue: the information printed for left and right hand side is identical</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">error: Tactic `rfl` failed: The left-hand side</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  @interior (E × E') instTopologicalSpaceProd (range (Prod.map ↑I ↑J))</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">is not definitionally equal to the right-hand side</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  @interior (E × E') instTopologicalSpaceProd (range (Prod.map ↑I ↑J))</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">E : Type u_1</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">E' : Type u_2</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">H : Type u_3</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">H' : Type u_4</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">inst✝⁵ : NormedAddCommGroup E</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">inst✝⁴ : NormedSpace ℝ E</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">inst✝³ : TopologicalSpace H</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">inst✝² : NormedAddCommGroup E'</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">inst✝¹ : NormedSpace ℝ E'</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">inst✝ : TopologicalSpace H'</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">I : ModelWithCorners ℝ E H</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">J : ModelWithCorners ℝ E' H'</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">⊢ interior (range (Prod.map ↑I ↑J)) = interior (range (Prod.map ↑I ↑J))</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×ˢ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">interior_prod_eq</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">range_prodMap</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">modelWithCorners_prod_coe</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">with_reducible</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 612574103,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784898505
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is a good point, although I am not sure yet what a consistently good error message could be.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trying to derive a reason for why the check failed from the defeq trace might be possible, but hard. The explanations could also get very long if the failure happens deep inside of a nested instance.</li>\n<li>We could use a more heuristic approach, such as making all the arguments explicit that look equal but aren't. For example, if the two <code>instTopologicalSpaceProd</code> aren't equal (syntactically? at a certain transparency?), they could be printed with <code>pp.explitit true</code> in order to expose the difference. There will probably still be some especially tricky cases that need closer inspection, and they might confuse by showing syntactic differences that are actually irrelevant for the failure. It might be worth a try.</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 612578723,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1784900002
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In this case, the different arguments are two types (<code>Prod H H'</code> and <code>ModelProd H H'</code>), which are implicit arguments to <code>Set.range</code>. Printing these arguments would have solved this case perfectly.</p>",
        "id": 612579023,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784900097
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In other words, even starting with \"print implicit type arguments if they are not equal at the configured transparency level\" would likely help quite a bit. Looking into differing instances... I'd do that as a second step.</p>",
        "id": 612579218,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1784900153
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm not aware with the implementation yet, but we already have some code that tries to make some parts explicit (I guess the <code>@</code> in your example might be a witness of that). In your case, as you say, it does seem it would profit from a more aggressive approach.</p>",
        "id": 612583182,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1784901309
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm pretty sure the problem is <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14143\">lean4#14143</a> here</p>",
        "id": 612600095,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1784905921
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I just also noticed that, but wasn't aware of that issue, thanks! I'm working on a fix that not only uses the correct transparency, but also the correct options. Otherwise, the errors would still be unhelpful when using <code>respectTransparency false</code>, for example.</p>",
        "id": 612600453,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1784906010
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"634338\">Michael Rothgang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/best.20practices.3A.20how.20to.20investigate.20non-reducible.20.60rfl.60/near/612574103\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"221653\">Paul Reichert</span> While you're here: for me, the proper fix would be to make the error message more detailed, by expanding implicit arguments as necessary. Here's an example depending on mathlib to illustrate my point:<br>\n[...]</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>With <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14537\">lean4#14537</a>, the error message in your example is:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`rfl</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">The</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">left</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">hand</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">side</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"n\">is</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">not</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">definitionally</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">equal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">right</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">hand</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">side</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ModelProd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_1</span>\n<span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_2</span>\n<span class=\"n\">H</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_3</span>\n<span class=\"n\">H'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_4</span>\n<span class=\"n\">inst</span><span class=\"bp\">✝⁵</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span>\n<span class=\"n\">inst</span><span class=\"bp\">✝⁴</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span>\n<span class=\"n\">inst</span><span class=\"bp\">✝³</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span>\n<span class=\"n\">inst</span><span class=\"bp\">✝²</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">inst</span><span class=\"bp\">✝¹</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">inst</span><span class=\"bp\">✝</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModelWithCorners</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H</span>\n<span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModelWithCorners</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">H'</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">⊢</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">interior</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It also no longer uses <code>@interior</code> because all implicit arguments of <code>interior</code> match.</p>",
